.TH CTI-VMSCAN 1 "2026-11-17" "cti-agent 1.6" "CTI Fleet Manual"
.SH NAME
cti-vmscan \- combine the week's Qualys VM scan-result notices into one report
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cti-vmscan
.RB [ \-\-since
.IR DURATION ]
.RB [ \-\-scans
.IR TITLES ]
.RB [ \-\-out
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-text\-out
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-subject\-out
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-seen\-out
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-coverage ]
.RB [ \-\-subnets\-file
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-csv\-out
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-fixes\-out
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-ticket\-slot ]
.PP
.B cti-vmscan \-\-stamp\-ticket
.I FILE
.B \-\-out
.I FILE
.B \-\-text\-out
.I FILE
.PP
.B cti-vmscan \-\-commit\-seen
.I FILE
.PP
.B sudo cti\-agent cti\-vmscan
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cti-vmscan
lists the VM scans launched over the last week from the Qualys API
(read-only), with the Asset Groups each targeted, and matches each to its
.B "Qualys: Scan Results"
notice in the CTI mailbox (Graph, read-only) by scan reference. It renders one
notice with every scheduled scan kept separate: that each ran, when and for how
long, how many addresses it scanned and how many were alive, and the locations
it covered (its Qualys Target Groups). A scan Qualys lists whose notice never
arrived is still reported and still counts for coverage, without host counts.
If the scan list cannot be read, the week is built from the notices alone and
the report says so. A scan launched by hand is listed, not counted.
.PP
It carries no vulnerability counts or severities, on purpose. The report goes
to people who do not act on them from here, and the two scans overlap, so the
counts would confuse more than they tell. The notice parser still reads them;
the report does not show them.
.SS The coverage check
It also checks the network spreadsheet (the source of authority for what
networks exist) against the Qualys Asset Groups the scans cover, by ADDRESS, not
by name, because the scans' Target Group names and the spreadsheet's location
names do not match. For each active location it takes its 10.X.30 network inside
the /16 the location's header names, whatever the row is labelled; with none, the
rows labelled
.BR corp ;
or whichever rows an operator rule names. A location is closed when its header
starts with DECOMMISSIONED (or the misspelling DECOMISSIONED). It reports:
.TP
.B NOT SCANNED
A location's network in no Asset Group, in a group no scan targeted this week, or only partly
inside the scanned groups. This is how a missed location shows up. The lane
records only that it is not scanned: why differs from one location to the next
(not set up yet, not reachable, no network architecture yet) and the security team
cannot check which, so the network and M&A teams are asked to say. The worksheet
has a blank column for their answer.
.TP
.B FIX OR CLARIFY IN THE SPREADSHEET
A location the check could not use: a corp row with no network written in it, a
corp network that is not a valid CIDR, one outside the location's own block (a
likely typo), one that overlaps another location's, or no row that clearly counts
as the corp network. The location is not checked until this is fixed, or an
rule in FILES says which rows count. The lane never guesses, and the fix is the
network team's: the security team only reads the spreadsheet.
.TP
.B CLEAN UP IN THE SPREADSHEET
What is wrong or unclear in the spreadsheet without stopping the check: two
locations listing overlapping networks, a row outside its location's block, the
same public IP under two locations, DECOMMISSIONED misspelled or not at the start
of a header, a header marked "??" or without its /16, a row with a VLAN but no
network, and the notes in FILES. Also for the network team, in the same ticket.
.TP
.B CHECK IN QUALYS
A Target Group a scan names that Qualys has no Asset Group for, a scanned Asset
Group whose addresses are only under decommissioned locations, a scanned group
whose addresses appear nowhere in the spreadsheet (or that has no IP ranges),
and every Asset Group with addresses that no scan targeted this week.
.PP
Every line names the location, the spreadsheet row to look at, and what to do, so
the report can go to the network and acquisitions teams as well as security. The
email ends with a short note on how the spreadsheet has to be laid out to be read
(a header row per location, the 10.X.30 network or a row labelled corp with a
CIDR, DECOMMISSIONED at the start of a retired location's header), and the same findings come as a worksheet
.RB ( \-\-csv\-out ,
attached by
.BR run\-vmscan ).
.PP
Decommissioned, free and open-to-use locations are skipped. If the check cannot
run (the spreadsheet or the Asset Groups cannot be read) the report says so; it
is never shown as a clean result. The check only reads: it changes nothing in
Qualys or in the spreadsheet.
.PP
It sends nothing. It is run by
.BR run\-vmscan (1),
which hands the files to
.BR cti\-mailer (1)
\-\-lane vmscan.
.PP
A scheduled scan that left no result in the window is the first thing the
report says, and its subject begins
.BR [NO\ RESULT] ;
a scan that did not finish is flagged
.BR [INCOMPLETE] .
.PP
A message with the results subject that did not come from Qualys is not read.
It is logged with a pointer to the usual cause, a schedule whose notification
From Address is set to this mailbox. Set it to qualys@qualys.net.
.PP
A message from Qualys's address is read only when Exchange Online
authenticated the sender: its own
.B Authentication\-Results
header records
.B compauth=pass
or
.B dmarc=pass
for the From domain. Anything else is logged as a problem and not read, and its
companion notices are not claimed. See
.BR FLEET_UNAUTHENTICATED_SENDERS .
.PP
Only the results notices of the expected scans are recorded as used, for the
mailbox cleanup to archive. For a scan that finished, its
.B Scan Completed
and
.B Completed Vulnerability Scan Notification
are recorded as well, for the cleanup to move to Deleted Items: they carry
nothing the report uses. Each must come from Qualys and match the run, by its
report reference or by an identical title and launch time; a scan that did not
finish keeps them in the inbox.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-check
Read-only: request a Graph token, read the network spreadsheet
.RB ( SUBNETS_DRIVE_ID ,
.BR SUBNETS_ITEM_ID ,
.BR SUBNETS_SHEET )
and find its sheet, list the Qualys Asset Groups once, list the VM scans
launched in the last seven days, and list the scan schedules: everything the
report and its coverage check need. It does not read the mailbox (the mailbox roles are
.BR "cti\-mailer \-\-check" 's),
writes nothing, and prints counts only, never a network, group or scan name,
because the heartbeat runs it. A coverage input that is not configured is a
failure, since that check is what holds this lane's timer off. Exits 0 when
all of it works, 1 otherwise.
.TP
.BI \-\-since " DURATION"
How far back to look for scan-result notices. Default 168h.
.TP
.BI \-\-scans " TITLES"
Comma-separated titles of scans to expect each week, ON TOP OF Qualys's active
scan schedules, which already say what should have run. Default
.BR VMSCAN_SCANS ,
else none; rarely needed. Each active schedule whose last launch fell in the
window, and each title here, that launched no scan is reported first as
.BR "[NO RESULT]" .
A schedule switched off is named, not reported missing.
Every scheduled scan that reported is included either way, and its Target
Groups count for the coverage check; a scan launched by hand is listed, not
counted. With no scan at all the subject says
.B [NO RESULT]
and the coverage check is not run. Titles are compared ignoring case and
spacing.
.TP
.BI \-\-out " FILE"
Write the HTML report here (mode 0600).
.TP
.BI \-\-text\-out " FILE"
Write the plain-text version here.
.TP
.BI \-\-subject\-out " FILE"
Write the subject line here, so the runner need not run a second time.
.TP
.BI \-\-seen\-out " FILE"
Write the notices this report covered here, as JSON, for
.BR \-\-commit\-seen .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-coverage
Skip the coverage check.
.TP
.BI \-\-subnets\-file " FILE"
Read the network spreadsheet (an .xlsx) from this file instead of SharePoint,
for a manual test.
.TP
.BI \-\-csv\-out " FILE"
Write the coverage findings as a worksheet (CSV) here: category, location, sheet
row, network, asset group and what to do. Written only when there is something to
work through. Cells that would open as a formula are made inert.
.TP
.BI \-\-fixes\-out " FILE"
Write the spreadsheet rows the network team has to correct (the FIX IN THE
SPREADSHEET findings) as JSON here, for
.B cti\-jira \-\-vmscan\-fixes
to file as one help-desk ticket. When the coverage check did not run the list
is marked held, and that ticket is left alone.
.TP
.B \-\-ticket\-slot
Leave a slot after the network team's sections of the coverage check (in the
HTML an invisible comment) for
.B \-\-stamp\-ticket
to fill with the spreadsheet ticket. The marker text arriving inside the
spreadsheet itself is removed first, so only the renderer's own slot is
filled.
.TP
.BI \-\-stamp\-ticket " FILE"
Fill the ticket slot in the
.B \-\-out
and
.B \-\-text\-out
reports already written, from this
.B "cti\-jira \-\-vmscan\-fixes \-\-ref\-out"
file, then exit: the ticket's key, status and what happened this week
("filed", "the list changed", "already has this exact list", "NOT updated"
and why), with a link to it when the link is a well-formed https Jira browse
URL for that key. A missing or unreadable
.I FILE
stamps "status unavailable"; a week with no ticket and nothing to correct
stamps nothing. Each report is replaced through a temporary file, so a failed
write leaves the unstamped report.
.B run\-vmscan
does this before the send.
.TP
.BI \-\-commit\-seen " FILE"
Record a
.B \-\-seen\-out
file in the VM scan lane's processed log, then exit. The mailbox cleanup reads
that log and archives those notices once they are 48 hours old.
.B run\-vmscan
does this only after a real send.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B VMSCAN_SCANS
Extra scan titles to expect, comma separated, on top of the scan schedules.
Optional and rarely needed.
.TP
.B FLEET_UNAUTHENTICATED_SENDERS
Exact From domains, comma separated, whose notices are read without the sender
authentication check. Only for a tenant whose inbound gateway hides the real
sender from Exchange Online, which makes every genuine notice fail. The domains
listed only; the first acceptance of each domain in a run is written to the
audit trail (even in a dry run: it is a trust decision, not a write). It replaces
.BR FLEET_SENDER_AUTH=off ,
which switched the check off for every sender and is no longer honoured.
.TP
.B SUBNETS_DRIVE_ID
.TQ
.B SUBNETS_ITEM_ID
Where the network spreadsheet lives in SharePoint (the drive and the file). The
Graph app registration needs permission to read that file as an application:
.B Sites.Selected
with a read grant on the site, or
.BR Files.Read.All .
.TP
.B SUBNETS_SHEET
The worksheet to read. Default
.BR Sites .
.TP
.B NETRULES_FILE
Optional security-team rules for locations the spreadsheet does not make clear. Default
.IR /etc/cti\-agent/netrules.txt .
.TP
.B NETNOTES_FILE
Optional notes from cross-checks, for the network team. Default
.IR /etc/cti\-agent/sheet\-notes.txt .
.TP
.B QUALYS_BASE_URL
.TQ
.B QUALYS_USERNAME
.TQ
.B QUALYS_PASSWORD
The Qualys API account, to list the Asset Groups (read-only).
.TP
.B FLEET_ORG
The organisation name in the report header.
.PP
The Graph settings are those of the other lanes
.RB ( TENANT_ID ,
.BR CLIENT_ID ,
.BR CLIENT_SECRET ,
.BR GRAPH_MAILBOX ).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/cti\-agent/netrules.txt
Optional, root-owned: the security team's own record about a location, one per line. The normal fix for a location the spreadsheet does not make clear is for the network team to correct the spreadsheet; this is only for what the security team decides to do meanwhile. A line is
.B location | use ROLE,ROLE
(these row labels count as its corp network) or
.B location | ignore
(leave it out of the check). The location is the start of its header in the
spreadsheet. Lines starting with # are comments.
.TP
.I /etc/cti\-agent/sheet\-notes.txt
Optional, root-owned: findings from checking the spreadsheet against other
records (an acquisition's network design, the incident-response contacts list),
one
.B location | what to check
per line. Each is listed for the network team in the clean-up section of the
report and of the spreadsheet ticket, until the line is deleted. The location
is the start of its header, or the name of a site the spreadsheet does not have
yet. Lines starting with # are comments.
.TP
.I $FLEET_HOME/state/processed\-vmscan.json
The notices this lane has used, written by
.BR \-\-commit\-seen .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The report was written. A missing or unfinished scan is in the report, not an
error.
.TP
.B 1
The mailbox could not be read, a file could not be written, or the processed
log does not parse. With
.BR \-\-check :
any of its checks failed.
.SH EXAMPLES
Render this week's report without sending it:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent cti\-vmscan \-\-out /tmp/vm.html \-\-text\-out /tmp/vm.txt
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR run\-vmscan (1),
.BR cti\-mailer (1),
.BR cti\-mailbox (1)
